which-direction","inspect-direction-within-the-ifn-associated-pattern","question-4-how-does-the-ifn-associated-pattern-vary-across-pbmc-identities","check-whether-the-direction-holds-across-pbmc-identities","pull-the-evidence-together","summary","synopsis","summary-plot","main-questions-revisited","continued-learning","core-homework","side-quests","optional-challenge","full-reproduction-guide","choose-what-you-want-to-rebuild","before-you-begin","complete-file-inventory","stage-1-confirm-the-environment-and-files","reproduction-roadmap","stage-2-rebuild-the-prepared-pbmc-dataset","the-script-starts-by-making-the-choices-visible","the-script-reads-the-source-anndata-file-and-preserves-counts","filtering-removes-genes-with-too-little-information","normalization-and-log-transformation-put-cells-on-a-comparable-scale","highly-variable-genes-define-the-modeling-space","the-script-writes-two-orientations-of-the-same-prepared-data","record-the-preprocessing-choices","check-the-prepared-dataset","stage-3-fit-the-selected-r-cogaps-model","run-the-model-where-you-can-see-its-progress","stage-4-create-diagnostic-and-analysis-tables","export-diagnostics-from-the-full-r-model","export-compact-r-tables-for-analysis","check-the-exported-tables-in-r-or-python","stage-5-rebuild-directionality-and-figures","rebuild-directionality-evidence","check-the-r-output-folder","regenerate-the-displayed-figures","stage-6-confirm-the-reproduction","optional-extension-rebuild-the-python-compatible-model","optional-extension-understand-how-the-rank-sweep-was-run","initial-sweep-order","lower-rank-and-longer-run-follow-ups","high-k-extension","script-library-read-the-complete-workflow","software-used-for-reproduction","r-and-bioconductor","python-53","rendering-figures-and-statistical-checks","troubleshooting-by-stage","docker-or-rstudio-server-does-not-start-correctly","preprocessing-cannot-find-or-create-its-files","the-selected-model-does-not-start-or-finish","the-model-finished-but-tables-or-figures-are-missing","cluster-commands-fail","finish-and-record-your-reproduction","additional-information","helpful-links","concepts-covered","glossary","references","session-info","acknowledgments"],"entries":[{"order":{"number":12,"section":[12,4,0,0,0,0,0]},"caption":"Paired donor check for the IFN-associated pattern. Each row compares one donor’s stimulated aliquot with that same donor’s control aliquot, using mean stimulated activity minus mean control activity. All eight donor pairs are positive, which supports the interpretation that the stimulation-associated increase is not driven by only one donor. This is a visual donor-aware check, not a substitute for a formal donor-level statistical model.","key":"fig-viz-ifn-replicate-pairs"},{"order":{"number":4,"section":[8,4,0,0,0,0,0]},"caption":"A labeled RStudio Server window showing where to paste R code, run selected lines, use the Terminal for Python or full scripts, and find output files.","key":"fig-rstudio-server-labeled-cs4"},{"order":{"number":15,"section":[12,7,0,0,0,0,0]},"caption":"Top-gene and directionality check for Pattern4, the IFN-associated pattern in the selected R run. The left panel ranks genes by CoGAPS weight, which tells you which genes most help define the pattern. The right panel checks whether those same genes are higher or lower in IFN-beta-stimulated cells compared with controls. Most top genes are canonical interferon-stimulated genes that are higher in stimulation, while FTH1 is a useful reminder that a high pattern weight does not automatically mean up-regulation.","key":"fig-viz-top-gene-directionality"},{"order":{"number":16,"section":[12,8,0,0,0,0,0]},"caption":"Directionality check for Pattern3, the dendritic / antigen-presentation-linked pattern in the selected R run. The left panel ranks top genes by CoGAPS weight. The middle panel compares expression in dendritic cells with other PBMC identities. The right panel compares IFN-beta stimulation with control. CD74 and HLA genes help define the pattern and are higher in dendritic cells, while most of those genes are not higher after IFN-beta stimulation.","key":"fig-viz-identity-pattern-directionality"},{"order":{"number":6,"section":[12,3,0,0,0,0,0]},"caption":"Pattern 1 is interpreted cautiously as an FCGR3A+ monocyte-linked pattern. The paired UMAP uses the same cells and coordinates in both panels: the left panel shows annotated PBMC identity neighborhoods, and the right panel recolors those cells by Pattern 1 activity. Activity is strongest in the FCGR3A+ monocyte neighborhood, stimulation association is weak, and high-weight genes include TIMP1, FTH1, MS4A7, FCGR3A, and SOD2.","key":"fig-viz-umap-pattern1"},{"order":{"number":5,"section":[12,2,0,0,0,0,0]},"caption":"Pattern-level screen for the selected R model. The left panel compares each pattern’s cell activity with the IFN-beta stimulation label, and the right panel compares mean activity in control and stimulated cells. Pattern 4 has the strongest positive stimulation association in this run, while other patterns show weaker positive, near-zero, or negative shifts. Use this plot as a guide for which pattern to inspect next, not as a complete biological interpretation by itself.","key":"fig-viz-pattern-condition-overview"},{"order":{"number":11,"section":[12,3,0,0,0,0,0]},"caption":"Pattern 6 is interpreted cautiously as a T-cell and baseline lymphocyte-linked pattern. The paired UMAP uses the same cells and coordinates in both panels: the left panel shows annotated PBMC identity neighborhoods, and the right panel recolors those cells by Pattern 6 activity. Activity is strongest in T-cell neighborhoods, is lower after stimulation, and high-weight genes include FTH1, FTL, ACTB, PFN1, and SH3BGRL3.","key":"fig-viz-umap-pattern6"},{"order":{"number":8,"section":[12,3,0,0,0,0,0]},"caption":"Pattern 3 is interpreted cautiously as a dendritic and antigen-presentation-linked pattern. The paired UMAP uses the same cells and coordinates in both panels: the left panel shows annotated PBMC identity neighborhoods, and the right panel recolors those cells by Pattern 3 activity. High-weight HLA genes and dendritic-cell activity point to antigen-presentation structure; top genes include CD74, HLA-DRA, HLA-DRB1, HLA-DPA1, and HLA-DPB1.","key":"fig-viz-umap-pattern3"},{"order":{"number":14,"section":[12,6,0,0,0,0,0]},"caption":"Condition shifts for every selected-model pattern across annotated PBMC identities. Each tile is computed as mean activity in IFN-beta-stimulated cells minus mean activity in control cells for one pattern and one cell type. Red positive values mean the pattern is stronger after stimulation. Blue negative values mean the pattern is stronger in control cells. The IFN-associated pattern shows positive shifts across several cell types, while other patterns show more cell-type-linked or negative behavior, reinforcing that the model is separating multiple overlapping sources of variation.","key":"fig-viz-pattern-celltype-shift"},{"order":{"number":7,"section":[12,3,0,0,0,0,0]},"caption":"Pattern 2 is interpreted cautiously as a CD14+ monocyte-linked pattern. The paired UMAP uses the same cells and coordinates in both panels: the left panel shows annotated PBMC identity neighborhoods, and the right panel recolors those cells by Pattern 2 activity. Activity is strongest in CD14+ monocytes, is lower after IFN-beta stimulation, and high-weight genes include FTH1, FTL, TIMP1, S100A11, and SH3BGRL3.","key":"fig-viz-umap-pattern2"},{"order":{"number":1,"section":[1,0,0,0,0,0,0]},"caption":"Type I and type III interferon biology in systemic lupus erythematosus. The diagram summarizes how self nucleic acids, immune complexes, neutrophil extracellular traps, interferons, and several immune-cell populations can participate in lupus-associated inflammatory feedback. Figure reproduced from Chyuan, Tzeng, and Chen (2019), Cells 8(9):963, licensed under CC BY 4.0. No content changes were made; the image is displayed at reduced size for teaching.","key":"fig-sle-ifn-pathogenesis"},{"order":{"number":13,"section":[12,5,0,0,0,0,0]},"caption":"Mean IFN-associated activity by annotated PBMC identity and condition. Each line connects the control mean to the IFN-beta-stimulated mean for one immune-cell type. The upward shift appears across most annotated PBMC identities, which supports a broad stimulation-associated signal, but the stimulated means are not identical across cell types. This combination is common in single-cell data: a shared response can still have different strength in different immune-cell populations.","key":"fig-viz-ifn-celltype-condition"},{"order":{"number":10,"section":[12,3,0,0,0,0,0]},"caption":"Pattern 5 is interpreted cautiously as a candidate secondary IFN and myeloid-response pattern. The paired UMAP uses the same cells and coordinates in both panels: the left panel shows annotated PBMC identity neighborhoods, and the right panel recolors those cells by Pattern 5 activity. Positive stimulation association and CXCL10/APOBEC3A genes suggest a second myeloid response signal; top genes include CXCL10, ISG15, APOBEC3A, IFITM3, and FTL.","key":"fig-viz-umap-pattern5"},{"order":{"number":3,"section":[8,1,0,0,0,0,0]},"caption":"Workflow map for the case study setup. The left column shows the main learner path: open prepared PBMC data, inspect model-resolution evidence, load selected CoGAPS outputs, check diagnostics, summarize activity, and interpret genes and direction. The upper-right column shows optional full local reproduction steps. The lower-right box shows the separate HPC sweep provenance path.","key":"fig-packages-setup-workflow-cs4"},{"order":{"number":2,"section":[8,1,0,0,0,0,0]},"caption":"Setup resources for the case study. Docker supplies the R and Python software environment, GitHub supplies the main learner files and lightweight CoGAPS evidence, optional Zenodo or local files support full local reproduction, and HPC sweep provenance is treated as a separate audit path.","key":"fig-packages-setup-resources-cs4"},{"order":{"number":9,"section":[12,3,0,0,0,0,0]},"caption":"Pattern 4 is the IFN-associated stimulation pattern in the selected R run. The paired UMAP uses the same cells and coordinates in both panels: the left panel shows annotated PBMC identity neighborhoods, and the right panel recolors those cells by Pattern 4 activity. Canonical interferon-stimulated genes and strong positive stimulation correlation mark the main IFN response; top genes include ISG15, ISG20, IFI6, IFIT3, and LY6E.","key":"fig-viz-umap-pattern4"}]}